<?xml version='1.0' ?>
<rrx namespace="ExportToDatabase" v="9.1.9.2">
  <com ref="Datacap.Libraries.ExportToDatabase.Actions"  qi="Saves batch data into a database.">
    <h>
        The ExportToDatabase action library used to export the page level and field level values into a table in the Database.
        Typically, exporting data to a database is the last step in a successful batch process.<br/><br/>
        
        The action DatabaseOpenConnection connects to a database using the connection string. 
        Subsequent export actions then use the open database connection for their work.        
        Similarly DatabaseCloseConnection will close the existing open connection.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>Note:</b> The ExportToDatabase Action library is 64-bit action library. 
        Ensure the 64 bit database (db2, Microsoft Access, SQL Server, Oracle) driver is installed properly.
        Microsoft Access is only supported for development in a single rulerunner environment. It is not supported for production.<br/><br/>
        
        The ExportToDatabase actions only support use within a single rulerunner. A profile using these actions
        must only be run by one rulerunner.<br/><br/>

        <b>SetTableName:</b><br/><br/>
        The action SetTableName confirms that the table name which is passed to action is present in database or not if not, then this action will return False.        
        This action must be called before exporting the any values into the table. 
        In the example below, the action checks if the Export Results table is present in the database or not.
        If not, then action returns false. If present, the action returns true and then processing moves to next action.<br/><br/>

        <b>SetTableName("Export_Results") </b><br/>
        ExportFieldToColumn("MyDate", "db_Date") <br/>
        AddRecord() <br/><br/>

        <b>ExportToColumn:</b><br/>
        ExportToColumn is a field-level action that exports the captured value of the current Field object from the page's Data file to a target column
        within a previously designated table of an open Export database.<br/><br/>

        <b>ExportFieldToColumn:</b><br/><br/>
        Action ExportFieldToColumn is a page-level action that extracts the captured value of a Field object from the Data file of the current page,
        and specifies its target location within a table of the Export database.<br/><br/>
        
        For Example:<br/>
        SetTableName("Export Results")<br/>
        <b>ExportFieldToColumn("VendorID","db_Number","2") </b><br/>
        <b>ExportFieldToColumn("Date","db_Date") </b><br/>
        <b>ExportFieldToColumn("Total","db_Total") </b><br/>
        AddRecord()<br/><br/>

        <b>AddRecord:</b><br/>
        The AddRecord action inserts assembled data into the database SetTableName("Invoice") <br/>
        ExportFieldToColumn("VendorID","db_Vendor")<br/>
        ExportFieldToColumn("Number","db_Number") <br/>
        ExportFieldToColumn("Total","db_Total") <br/>
        <b>AddRecord()</b><br/><br/><br/>

        
        <b>⟸ ♕ Generating Statistics ♕ ⟹</b><br/><br/>

        The statistics actions are used to calculate the page classification count and recognition count. The counts are stored in runtime DCO.
        The statistics actions utilize the DatabaseOpenConnection and DatabaseCloseConnection actions,
        but do not require the use of any of the other export actions.<br/><br/>

        There are situations where classification or recognized text changes conditionally.
        For example, recognized field text can be changed by "cleaning" rulesets or
        by a verification operator.
        This action library identifies that values that has been changed and counts them
        counted as recognition errors or page classification errors.<br/><br/>

        The page type must be set, and a recognition action must be called, and field values populated, prior to calling
        any of these actions Recognize action should be called before calling any of these actions.
        SaveFieldsRecognizedText must be called prior to any cleaning actions or user verification.<br/><br/>

        The saving of statistics data can be controlled in the application manager.
        The check box <b>Save Statistics</b> can be checked or unchecked, which sets the application manager entry "SaveReportStatistics".
        This value can be then used in the ruleset to control the call of Statistics actions.<br/><br/>

        For Example:<br/>
        rrCompareCase("@APPVAR(SaveReportStatistics)", "True", "False")<br/>
        SaveFieldsText("")<br/><br/>

        In the example, the application logic first checks if the application is configured
        to save statistics information.  If so, it then calls the statistics action.<br/><br/>

        The following are the typical logical steps performed when using the statistics actions. These
        steps are not all performed in a single task:<br/><br/>

        1. Perform recognition and populate fields.<br/>        
        2. Call the action StatisticsSaveFieldsText.<br/>
        3. The verify operator has performed verification on page types and field recognition results.<br/>
        4. Call the action StatisticsCompareFieldsText.<br/>
        5. Call the action DatabaseOpenDatabase.<br/>
        6. Perform any necessary actions to export batch data using the ExportToDatabase actions.<br/>
        7. Call the action StatisticsAddToDatabaseTotals.<br/>
        8. Call the action ExportCloseDatabase.<br/><br/><br/>


        <b>⟸ ♕ The Datacap Document Object Hierarchy (DCO) ♕ ⟹</b><br/><br/>
        
        Applications are created with a "Setup DCO" and each batch has a unique "Runtime DCO".
        The DCO is explained in the top-level help of the ApplicationObject action library.<br/><br/><br/>
        

        <b>⟸ ♕ Smart Parameters ♕ ⟹</b><br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are a syntactic way of specifying action parameters that are resolved at runtime,
        allowing dynamic input of data to actions.  The complete smart parameter reference is available
        in the top-level help of the RuleRunnerLogic action library.<br/><br/>
    </h>

      <method name="AddRecord" qi="Inserts assembled data into the database table specified by a previous SetTableName action.">
          <ap>None. </ap>
          <h>
              Inserts assembled data into the database table specified by a previous <b>SetTableName</b> action.<br/><br/>

              <b>Important:</b> This action must be placed after earlier actions that open the database, gather data, and access the correct table.<br/><br/>
              
              <e>
                  SetTableName("Invoice")<br/>
                  ExportFieldToColumn("VendorID","db_Vendor")<br/>
                  ExportFieldToColumn("Number","db_Number")<br/>
                  ExportFieldToColumn("Total","db_Total")<br/>
                  <b>AddRecord()</b><br/><br/>

                  This ExportDB rule applies to a Page object of the Document Hierarchy.<br/><br/>

                  The actions open the database and direct the rule's attention to the Invoice table.
                  The rule then sets up a record with three values - Vendor ID, Invoice Number and Total.
                  Afterwards, the AddRecord action updates the table with new information. <br/><br/>

                  Note: A return of False means the record may not have been successfully exported,
                  and needs to be followed by a "failure check" rule that typically would call
                  SetTaskStatus(0), or the RuleRunnerLogic action SetTaskAbortBatch, to ensure that the batch aborts.<br/><br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>All, but generally used at the Page or Field level.</lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False</b> if there is no connection to the database; if an error occurs when the action
              attempts to add the record to the database; or if a SetTableName action was not previously used.<br/><br/>
              Otherwise, <b>True</b>.
          </ret>
          <see>
              SetTableName, ExportFieldToColumn
          </see>
      </method>

      <method name="DatabaseCloseConnection" qi="Closes an open connection to your Export database.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Closes an open connection to the previously opened Export database. <br/><br/>

        Usually, this action is placed in a ruleset that is separate from the ruleset that opens
        the connection and stores the data. <br/><br/>

        For example, this action could be placed into a ruleset called ExportDBClose,
        and attached to a batch level close event which executes after all data has been
        exported from the batch to the specified database.<br/><br/>
          
        <e>
          <b>DatabaseCloseConnection()</b><br/><br/>

          This action closes the previously opened connection to the Export database. <br/><br/>

          This action is usually part of a separate ruleset that prevents the need to repeatedly
          open the connection to the Export database. (You can open the connection once in the first
          ruleset, export data from all documents and pages in the batch, then close the connection once
          in the second ruleset.)  The database must be opened, used and closed all within
          the same task profile.  You cannot open the connection in one task profile and then
          expect to use the connection in a different task profile.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally used as part of a separate ruleset at the Batch level. </lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True, </b> even if the connection is already closed.
      </ret>
      <see>DatabaseOpenConnection</see>


    </method>

    <method name="DatabaseOpenConnection" qi="Opens a connection to the database specified as the parameter.">
      <p name="strConnectionString" type="string"/>
      <ap>
          The Connection String of the target Export database.
          Use smart parameters to retrieve the connection from the application manager
          to avoid clear text passwords in your application.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Opens a connection to the database specified as the parameter. <br/><br/>

        A rule containing this action can apply to any object of the Document Hierarchy (DCO), but it is most often used at the batch level. <br/><br/>
          
          This action connects to the database using a 64 bit connection.  
          The proper 64 bit client interface must be available for the target database.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>DatabaseOpenConnection("@APPVAR(*/exportdb:cs)")</b><br/><br/>

          <b>Note:</b> This action must come before any other ExportToDatabase actions.  This example
          uses smart parameters to obtain the connection string from the application service
          where the custom connection string key "exportdb" has been created in the "Custom Values" tab.
          It provides a single location for the connection string and security for passwords. 
          If necessary, multiple custom variables can be created to connect to different databases.<br/><br/>

          <b>Note:</b> If the action is establishing a connection with an Oracle database,
          or an SQL Server database using SQL Server Authentication, be sure to expand the DSN parameter
          by adding the correct Provider, user ID and Password.  For example:<br/><br/>

            <b>Access:</b><br/>
            DatabaseOpenConnection("Provider=Microsoft.ACE.OLEDB.12.0;Data Source=C:\Datacap\TravelDocs\TravelDocsExport.mdb;Persist Security Info=False;")<br/><br/>
            
            Access databases are not supported in a production environment.<br/><br/>

            <b>Oracle:</b><br/>
            DatabaseOpenConnection("PROVIDER=OraOLEDB.Oracle;Data Source=1040Look;ID=Admin;Password=Admin;")<br/><br/>

            <b>SQL Server:</b><br/>
            DatabaseOpenConnection("PROVIDER=ODBCMSSQL;DSN=1040Look;CATALOG=;DBNTA=;UID=Admin;PWD=Admin;")<br/><br/>

            <b>DB2:</b><br/>
            Provider=IBMDADB2;Data Source=MYDB2SOURCE;UID=user;PWD=password<br/><br/>

            <b>Note:</b> Although the examples show the full connection string, it is recommended that a smart
            parameter is used to obtain the connection string from the application service, as shown in the first example,
            to provide password security and application portability.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally used at the Batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the connection opens. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        DatabaseCloseConnection
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetTableName" qi="Sets the name of the table in your database to which the data is to be exported.">
      <p name="TableName" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <b>TableName.</b> The name of the database table where the exported data will be inserted.<br/><br/>
        The action confirm that the supplied table name is valid.  If the table name is not valid, SetTableName will return False.
        Note that this value causes an extra database call so it is typically specified only during development,
        not in a production system as it will increase the number of database calls and running time.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Sets the name of the table in your database to which the data is to be exported.  This action needs to be used before the AddRecord action.<br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetTableName("Export_Results")</b><br/>
          ExportFieldToColumn("MyDate", "db_Date")<br/>
          AddRecord()<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Returns <b>False</b> only if the first parameter contains an invalid table name and a value of 1 is specified as a second parameter.
        Otherwise, this action always returns <b>True. </b>
      </ret>
      <see>AddRecord</see>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportBatchIDToColumn" qi="Stores the current batch ID into the database.">
      <p name="Column" type="string"/>
      <p name="SQLFormatValue" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Column.</b> The name of the database column that will hold the value.<br/><br/>

        <b>SQLFormatValue.</b> Optional: Can be set to  the numerical value of 1, 2, or 3.<br/><br/>

        If this parameter is not specified, quotes will be placed around the value in the SQL query to update the table. <br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 1 causes this action to return False if the Table name is invalid;
        otherwise, the error will not be discovered until the action that performs the DB insert call,
        such as an AddRecord action, is called.  This value would typically
        only be used in a development environment as it will increase processing time.<br/><br/>

        Setting this value to 2 allows NULL column values and inserts no quotes around values (for numeric) in the SQL query to update the database. <br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 3 allows NULL and if the column value is not NULL,
        the column value will be surrounded in quotes in the SQL query to update the database.

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
        <br/><br/>
      </ap>

      <h>
        Exports the current Batch ID to the database column specified by the parameter. The action also has an optional query format parameter with two values.<br/><br/>
          
        <e>
          SetTableName("Export_Results")<br/>
          <b>ExportBatchIDToColumn("db_BatchID","1")</b><br/>
          ExportFieldToColumn("Date","db_Date")<br/>
          ExportFieldToColumn("Total","db_Total")<br/>
          AddRecord()<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally at the Page or Field level. </lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the second parameter is set to "1" and the parameters do not identify a
        valid database column. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>AddRecord, ExportFieldToColumn, ExportPropertyToColumn</see>

    </method>

    <method name="ExportToColumn" qi="A field-level action that exports the captured value of the current Field object from the page's Data file to a target column within a previously designated table of an open Export database.">
      <p name="Column" type="string"/>
      <p name="SQLFormatValue" type="string"/>
      <ap>
          <b>Column. </b> The name of the database column that will hold the value of the current field. <br/><br/>

          <b>SQLFormatValue.</b> Optional: Can be set to  the numerical value of 1, 2, or 3.  <br/>

          If this parameter is not specified, quotes will be placed around the value in the SQL query to update the table. <br/><br/>
          Setting the value to 1 causes this action to return False if the Table name is invalid;
          otherwise, the error will not be discovered until the action that performs the DB insert call,
          such as an AddRecord action, is called.  This value would typically only be used
          in a development environment as it will increase processing time. <br/><br/>

          Setting this value to 2 allows NULL column values and inserts no quotes around values (for numeric) in the SQL query to update the database. <br/><br/>

          Setting the value to 3 allows NULL and if the column value is not NULL, the column value will be
          surrounded in quotes in the SQL query to update the database. <br/><br/>
          
          Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This field-level action exports the captured value of the current Field object from the page's
        Data file to a target column within a previously designated table of an open Export database.
        Optionally, it can assign a query format to the action.<br/><br/>
          
        <e>
          Batch Level:<br/>
          DatabaseOpenConnection("@APPVAR(*/exportdb:cs)")<br/>
          SetTableName("Export_Results”) <br/><br/>

          Current field:<br/>
          <b>ExportToColumn("db_Date","3”) </b><br/><br/>

          Last Field:<br/>
          AddRecord()<br/><br/>

          This example exports the captured value of the Field object to which the rule applies,
          from the Data file of the current page to the db_Date column of the Export_ Results table,
          within the Export database.<br/>          
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level only. </lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if:<br/>
        <b>1.</b> There is no connection to the database.<br/>
        <b>2.</b> The column identified by the parameters does not exist.<br/>
        <b>3.</b> A SetTableName action was not previously used.<br/><br/>

        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>ExportFieldToColumn, ExportPropertyToColumn</see>

    </method>

    <method name="ExportFieldToColumn" qi="A page-level action that extracts the captured value of a Field object from the Data file of the current page, and specifies its target location within a table of the Export database.">
      <p name="Field" type="string"/>
      <p name="Column" type="string"/>
      <p name="SQLFormatValue" type="string"/>
      <ap>

        <b>Field. </b> The name of the field whose value will be stored in the column.<br/><br/>

        <b>Column. </b> The name of the database column that will hold the value.<br/><br/>

        <b>SQLFormatValue.</b> Optional: Can be set to  the numerical value of 1, 2, or 3.  If this parameter is not specified,
        quotes will be placed around the value in the SQL query to update the table.<br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 1 causes this action to return False if the Table name is invalid;
        otherwise, the error will not be discovered until the action that performs the DB insert call,
        such as an AddRecord action, is called.  This value would typically only be
        used in a development environment as it will increase processing time. <br/><br/>

        Setting this value to 2 allows NULL column values and inserts no quotes around values
        (for numeric) in the SQL query to update the database. <br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 3 allows NULL and if the column value is not NULL,
        the column value will be surrounded in quotes in the SQL query to update the database.<br/><br/>


        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This is a page-level action that extracts the captured value of a Field object from the
        Data file of the current page, and specifies its target location within a table of the Export database.<br/><br/>

        Within a rule, this action should run before an AddRecord action, which commits the data
        to the database.
        <e>
          SetTableName("Export_Results")<br/>
          <b>
            ExportFieldToColumn("VendorID","db_Number","2")<br/>
            ExportFieldToColumn("Date","db_Date")<br/>
            ExportFieldToColumn("Total","db_Total")
          </b><br/>
          AddRecord()<br/><br/>

          This action exports the captured value of three Field objects from the Data file of the current page,
          to corresponding columns of the Export Results table, within the open Export database.<br/><br/>

          <b>Important:</b> DatabaseOpenConnection action must have been previously called to establish a connection
          to your Export database.  This is usually accomplished by a rule at the Batch level.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level only.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if:<br/>
        <b>1.</b> There is no connection to the database.<br/>
        <b>2.</b> The database column specified as a parameter does not exist.<br/>
        <b>3.</b> The Field object identified by the parameter does not exist.<br/>
        <b>4.</b> A SetTableName action was not used previously.<br/><br/>

        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>SetTableName, ExportToColumn</see>

    </method>

    <method name="ExportSmartParamToColumn" qi="Adds the evaluated value of a smart parameter to a column of the Export database">
      <p name="SmartField" type="string"/>
      <p name="Column" type="string"/>
      <p name="SQLFormatValue" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <b>SmartField. </b> A smart parameter that specifies the value to be stored into the column.<br/><br/>

        <b>Column.</b> The name of the database column that will hold the value.<br/><br/>

        <b>SQLFormatValue. </b> Optional: Can be set to  the numerical value of 1, 2, or 3.<br/><br/>

        If this parameter is not specified, quotes will be placed around the value in the SQL query to update the table. <br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 1 causes this action to return False if the Table name is invalid;
        otherwise, the error will not be discovered until the action that performs the DB insert call,
        such as an AddRecord action, is called.  This value would typically only be used
        in a development environment as it will increase processing time.<br/><br/>

        Setting this value to 2 allows NULL column values and inserts no quotes
        around values (for numeric) in the SQL query to update the database.<br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 3 allows NULL and if the column value is not NULL,
        the column value will be surrounded in quotes in the SQL query to update the database.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters<br/><br/>
        <br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Using the database opened by an earlier ExportDB action, this action will
        store a value into the specified column for the current row.
        <br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ExportSmartParamToColumn("@P\MyField.TYPE","EXPDBCOLUM")</b>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally at the Page or Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if:<br/><br/>
        <b>1.</b> There is no connection to the database.<br/>
        <b>2.</b> The column identified by the parameter does not exist.<br/>
        <b>3.</b> A <b>SetTableName</b> action was not previously used.<br/><br/>
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>

    </method>

    <method name="ExportNodeXMLToColumn" qi="Exports the value of the XML property of the bound object (node) of the Document Hierarchy to a column of the Export database.">
      <p name="SmartParameter Path" type="string"/>
      <p name="Column" type="string"/>
      <p name="SQLFormatValue" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <br/><br/>
        <b>SmartParameter Path.</b> The smart parameter path to the bound object of the Document Hierarchy.
        This object's XML property will be added to a designated column of the Export database.<br/><br/>

        <b>Column.</b> The String value of the name of the target column in the Export database.
        The action will add the value of the calling object's XML property to this column.<br/><br/>

        <b>SQLFormatValue.</b> Optional: Can be set to the numerical value of 1 or 3.  <br/><br/>

        If this parameter is not specified, quotes will be placed around the value in the SQL query to update the table.<br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 1 causes this action to return False if the Table name is invalid;
        otherwise, the error will not be discovered until the action that performs the
        DB insert call, such as an AddRecord action, is called.  This value would typically
        only be used in a development environment as it will increase processing time.<br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 3 allows NULL and if the column value is not NULL, the column value<br/><br/>
        will be surrounded in quotes in the SQL query to update the database.<br/><br/>
        Smart parameters are  supported  all parameters<br/><br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action exports the value of the <b>XML</b> property of the bound object (node)
        of the Document Hierarchy to a column of the Export database.<br/>
        <br/>
        <e>
          <b>ExportNodeXMLToColumn("@P\MyField","MYDBCOLUM")</b>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally at the Page or Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if:<br/>
        <br/>
        <b>1.</b> There is no connection to the database.<br/>
        <b>2.</b> The column identified by the parameter does not exist.<br/>
        <b>3.</b> A <b>SetTableName</b> action was not previously used.<br/>
        <b>4.</b> The smart parameter path does not point to a valid object of the Document Hierarchy.<br/>
                  If the smart parameter path is invalid then the Null/Blank value get inserted into table and action returns true.<br/><br/>
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportPropertyToColumn" qi="Adds the value of a property (variable) of the selected object to a column of the Export database">
      <p name="PropertyName" type="string"/>
      <p name="Column" type="string"/>
      <p name="SQLFormatValue" type="string"/>
      <ap>
        <br/>
        <b>PropertyName.</b> The property name whose value will be stored in the column.<br/><br/>

        <b>Column.</b> The name of the database column that will hold the value.<br/><br/>

        <b>SQLFormatValue.</b> Optional: Can be set to  the numerical value of 1, 2, or 3.  <br/><br/>

        If this parameter is not specified, quotes will be placed around the value in the SQL query to update the table.<br/><br/>
        Setting the value to 1 causes this action to return False if the Table name is invalid;
        otherwise, the error will not be discovered until the action that performs the DB
        insert call, such as an AddRecord action, is called.  This value would
        typically only be used in a development environment as it will increase processing time.<br/><br/>

        Setting this value to 2 allows NULL column values and inserts no quotes around values
        (for numeric) in the SQL query to update the database.<br/><br/>

        Setting the value to 3 allows NULL and if the column value is not NULL,
        the column value will be surrounded in quotes in the SQL query to update the database.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters<br/><br/>

      </ap>
      <h>
        Adds the value of a property (variable) of the selected object to a column of the Export database.
        <e>
          SetTableName("Export_Results")<br/>
          ExportFieldToColumn("VendorID","db_Number")<br/>
          <b>ExportPropertyToColumn("Status","db_PageStatus","2")</b><br/>
          ExportFieldToColumn("Total","db_Total")<br/>
          AddRecord()<br/><br/>

          This sequence updates the db_PageStatus column of the Export Results table with the value of
          the selected object's Status property. It also uses the optional third parameter to define
          a <i>Style</i> for the action.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All, but generally used at the Page or Field level. </lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if:<br/><br/>
        <b>1.</b> There is no connection to the Export database.<br/>
        <b>2.</b> The column of the database does not exist.<br/>
        <b>3.</b> The property (variable) identified by the parameter does not exist.<br/>
        <b>4. </b> A SetTableName action was not used previously.<br/><br/>

        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>ExportToColumn, ExportFieldToColumn, ExportBatchIDToColumn</see>
    </method>


      <method name="StatisticsAddToDatabaseTotals" qi="Calculates page type classification and field recognition accuracy statistics.">          
          <ap>
              None.
          </ap>
          <h>
              Updates the application summary statistics in the reportTotal table with this batch's totals.  
              Once this action executes successfully it sets batch variable TotalStatsUpdated to avoid double-counting.<br/><br/>

              DatabaseOpenConnection must be called prior to using this action. ExportCloseConnection must be called after all export database
              actions have completed in the current task profile.  If a connection to the database has not been made,
              this action will attempt to open the database using the default database connection "@APPVAR(values/dsn/exportdb:cs)"
              but the action ExportCloseConnection must still be called in the same task profile,
              once the database is no longer needed.<br/><br/>

              Before StatisticsAddToDatabaseTotals updates the database, it checks the DCO variable TotalStatsUpdated to confirm that statistics
              have not yet been saved for the current batch.
              Once this action executes successfully it sets batch variable TotalStatsUpdated to avoid double-counting within the database.<br/><br/>
              
              This action saves the data in Application Engine Database ReportTotal table.
              The database column rt_featureCount1 and rt_TotalSizeKB are updated by this action.<br/><br/>
              
              The field rt_featureCount1 will be updated only when there is a change observed in the recognition of page or fields.<br/><br/>
              
              The rt_TotalSizeKB field will store the size of Batch Directory.<br/><br/>
              
              This action does not perform any updates in the reportBatch table.<br/><br/>              
              <br /><br />
              <e>
                  <b>StatisticsAddToDatabaseTotals()</b>
                  <br/>
                  <br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Batch.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              <b>False,</b> if the database connection does not exist or if an error occurs.  Otherwise, <b>True.</b><br/><br/>
              
              If the database connection does not exist, the batch will be set to the "Abort" state.<br/><br/>
          </ret>
          <see>
              StatisticsCompareFieldsText, StatisticsSaveFieldsText.<br/><br/>
              
              Refer to the top-level help of this action library for general information about exporting batch statistics.<br/>
          </see>
      </method>

      <method name="StatisticsCompareFieldsText" qi="Calculates page type classification and field recognition accuracy statistics.">
          <p name="SaveStatusVariableName" type="string" qi="Optional. If provided, the action will also save the object status using the variable name provided."/>
          <ap>
              Smart parameters are supported.
          </ap>
          <h>
              Call this action after data verification to calculate accuracy based on the changes to data during verification.
              The StatisticsSaveFieldsText action must have previously run after the recognition has been performed, but before operator verification.
              If the verify operator has corrected any of the recognized fields, this action will count the number of fields that have been changed.
              The saved values that were generated by StatisticsSaveFieldsText, are compared with the values that exist after operator verification, 
              calculating accuracy statistics.<br/><br/>

              All pages within the batch, and all fields on all pages, are processed by this action.<br/><br/>

              Batch variables are populated with accuracy values:<br/><br/>

              PageClassifiedErrors: the number of page classification errors (page type changed after auto-classification).<br/>
              PageClassifiedCount: the number of pages classified in this batch.<br/>
              FieldRecognitionCount: the number of fields with recognized text.<br/>
              FieldRecognitionErrors: the number of fields that were incorrectly recognized (text values were changed after recognition).<br/>
              FieldAccuracyPct: Field recognition accuracy percentage.<br/>
              ClassifyAccuracyPct: Classification accuracy percentage.<br/><br/>

              The action processes all pages and fields to calculate the values of the listed variables but takes no further action.
              This action does not
              have any interaction with the database, it simply prepares data for later processing, saving results in batch level variables.
              It is not necessary to open the connection to the export database before using this action.<br/><br/>

              The action compares the original page type classification with the final value after verification. Any differences that have
              occurred are counted. The calculation will be done for only those pages which were classified correctly and
              have a variable <b>ClassifiedPageType</b> in DCO xml added by the action SaveFieldsRecognizedText.
              <br/><br/>
              
              Following are the variables added in runtime DCO file by this action:<br/>
              <ul>
                  <li>
                      <b>PageClassifiedErrors :</b>
                  </li>
                  The variable contains the page classification errors count (page type changed after auto-classification).<br/>
                  It compares the saved value in ClassifiedPageType with the current type of page.
                  If the value of the variable and type of page is different it is assumed the page type is changed after auto-classification and the count of PageClassifiedErrors variable will be increased.<br/>
                  <li>
                      <b>PageClassifiedCount :</b>
                  </li>
                  The variable stores the total number of pages classified in this batch.<br/>
                  The count compares the saved value in ClassifiedPageType which was added by the action SaveFieldsRecognizedText.
                  The page type classified as Other is considered as not classified or incorrectly classified.<br/>
                  <li>
                      <b>FieldRecognitionCount :</b>
                  </li>
                  The variable stores the total number of fields that were recognized.<br/>
                  The count is based on the variable RecognizedText, that was added by a previous call to the action SaveFieldsRecognizedText.<br/>
                  <li>
                      <b>FieldRecognitionErrors :</b>
                  </li>
                  The variable stores the number of fields that were incorrectly recognized, meaning it was changed after recognition.<br/>
                  It compares the value in variable RecognizedText with the current field object text.
                  If values are different, it is assumed that the value of the current field object is changed after recognition and the count of the variable FieldRecognitionErrors will be increased.<br/>
                  <li>
                      <b>FieldAccuracyPct :</b>
                  </li>
                  Field Accuracy Percentage calculates the percentage of field recognition accuracy using the value of the variable FieldRecognitionCount and FieldRecognitionErrors.<br/>
                  <li>
                      <b>ClassifyAccuracyPct : </b>
                  </li>
                  Classify Accuracy Percentage calculates the percentage of page classification accuracy using the value of the variable PageClassifiedCount and PageClassifiedErrors.<br/>
              </ul>
              <br/><br/>

              This action must be called as a final step before submitting the results to the database with the action StatisticsAddToDatabaseTotals.
              <br/><br/>              
              <e>
                  <b>StatisticsCompareFieldsText("")</b><br/><br/>

                  These are the logical steps that must be performed in previous tasks before calling StatisticsCompareFieldsText()<br/><br/>

                  <ul>
                      <li>
                          Perform recognition and populate fields.<br/>
                      </li>
                      <li>
                          Call the action StatisticsSaveFieldsText.<br/>
                      </li>
                      <li>
                          The verify operator has performed verification on page types and field recognition results.<br/>
                      </li>
                  </ul>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Batch.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>StatisticsSaveFieldsText</see>
      </method>
      

      <method name="StatisticsSaveFieldsText" qi="Stores the current recognition and page classification results prior to verification.">
          <ap>None.</ap>
          <h>
              Call this action after page classification and OCR recognition, but before verification, to save the original recognized data for statistics calculations.
              Information is stored within page and field variables allowing the contents to be saved or later compared to the results after verification.<br/><br/>

              All pages within the batch, and all fields on all pages, are processed by this action.<br/><br/>

              The classification type of each page is stored in page variable "ClassifiedPageType".
              The recognized text value of each field is stored in field variable "RecognizedText".
              In a second step, in a later profile, these variables will be checked by the action StatisticsCompareFieldsText
              and compared to the current values to determine if the verification operator has changed
              any page type classifications or has updated recognized field text on each page.<br/><br/>

              This action does not
              perform any interaction with the database, it simply prepares data for later processing, saving results in batch level variables.
              It is not necessary to open the connection to the export database before using this action.<br/><br/>

              <e>
                  <b>StatisticsSaveFieldsText()</b><br/>
                  <br/>
              </e>
          </h>
          <lvl>
              Batch.
          </lvl>
          <ret>
              Always <b>True.</b>
          </ret>
          <see>StatisticsCompareFieldsText</see>
      </method>

  </com>
</rrx>


